WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.664
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Cambodia Grade twelve English.

2
00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:09.269
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.569 --> 00:00:18.038
Unit fifteen: Reading Skills: Inference and Vocabulary in Context. Today you will understand ideas that are not stated directly.

4
00:00:18.338 --> 00:00:21.095
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:22.033 --> 00:00:25.011
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:26.898
Inference.

7
00:00:27.883 --> 00:00:30.289
Inference means a conclusion from clues.

8
00:00:30.883 --> 00:00:32.326
Make an inference.

9
00:00:32.917 --> 00:00:35.488
Your turn. Say: Inference.

10
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:39.439
Inference.

11
00:00:40.183 --> 00:00:41.301
Imply.

12
00:00:42.317 --> 00:00:44.757
Imply means to suggest without saying.

13
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:47.210
The writer implies a problem.

14
00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:50.295
Your turn. Say: Imply.

15
00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:54.297
Imply.

16
00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:55.807
Tone.

17
00:00:56.817 --> 00:00:58.828
Tone means the writer's attitude.

18
00:00:59.417 --> 00:01:00.998
The tone is serious.

19
00:01:01.583 --> 00:01:03.725
Your turn. Say: Tone.

20
00:01:06.567 --> 00:01:07.324
Tone.

21
00:01:08.083 --> 00:01:08.922
Purpose.

22
00:01:09.917 --> 00:01:11.928
Purpose means the reason for writing.

23
00:01:12.517 --> 00:01:13.854
What is the purpose?

24
00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.056
Your turn. Say: Purpose.

25
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:20.866
Purpose.

26
00:01:21.617 --> 00:01:22.629
Context.

27
00:01:23.617 --> 00:01:26.092
Context means words around a word.

28
00:01:26.683 --> 00:01:28.184
Use the context.

29
00:01:28.783 --> 00:01:31.273
Your turn. Say: Context.

30
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:35.309
Context.

31
00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:37.156
Paraphrase.

32
00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.475
Paraphrase means to say in other words.

33
00:01:41.083 --> 00:01:42.793
Paraphrase the sentence.

34
00:01:43.383 --> 00:01:45.827
Your turn. Say: Paraphrase.

35
00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:49.737
Paraphrase.

36
00:01:50.483 --> 00:01:51.484
Reference.

37
00:01:52.483 --> 00:01:54.843
Reference means a word that points back.

38
00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:57.299
It is a reference to the city.

39
00:01:57.883 --> 00:02:00.164
Your turn. Say: Reference.

40
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.071
Reference.

41
00:02:04.817 --> 00:02:05.655
Conclude.

42
00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.114
Conclude means to decide from evidence.

43
00:02:09.717 --> 00:02:11.484
I conclude that he is right.

44
00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:14.666
Your turn. Say: Conclude.

45
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:18.559
Conclude.

46
00:02:19.367 --> 00:02:23.110
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:26.066
Visal, what is an inference question?

48
00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:36.815
It asks about something the text suggests but does not say directly. You must use clues in the text, such as the writer's words and examples, to decide.

49
00:02:37.917 --> 00:02:39.777
How do I guess unknown words?

50
00:02:40.883 --> 00:02:52.557
Look at the words around it. Decide whether it is a noun, verb or adjective, check for a prefix or suffix, and test whether your guess makes sense in the sentence.

51
00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:55.475
What about the writer's purpose?

52
00:02:56.583 --> 00:03:09.243
Ask whether the writer wants to inform, persuade or entertain. Strong opinions and commands often show persuasion, and facts and numbers show information.

53
00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:11.819
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:12.683 --> 00:03:15.066
Grammar focus: clues to inference.

55
00:03:15.366 --> 00:03:16.530
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:17.217 --> 00:03:19.890
The text implies that the road was dangerous.

57
00:03:20.783 --> 00:03:22.713
I conclude that he was tired.

58
00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:30.438
Use clues in the text to infer meaning, guess unknown words from context and decide the writer's purpose from tone and content.

59
00:03:31.183 --> 00:03:34.509
Be careful! Don't say: I use my own opinion.

60
00:03:36.467 --> 00:03:38.849
Say: I use clues from the text.

61
00:03:39.149 --> 00:03:40.417
Use evidence.

62
00:03:41.233 --> 00:03:44.060
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:44.867 --> 00:03:46.541
Reading Between the Lines.

64
00:03:46.841 --> 00:03:49.723
A writer describes a village road after the storm.

65
00:03:50.733 --> 00:03:52.884
The road was full of holes and mud.

66
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.817
Two drivers refused to travel until it was repaired.

67
00:03:57.833 --> 00:04:00.634
The text implies that the road was dangerous.

68
00:04:01.633 --> 00:04:04.179
The writer wants readers to demand better roads.

69
00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:09.361
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:10.217 --> 00:04:12.332
An inference is not stated blank.

71
00:04:12.632 --> 00:04:15.752
A, directly. B, at all. C, ever.

72
00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.404
The answer is A: directly.

73
00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:24.468
Guess unknown words from the blank.

74
00:04:24.768 --> 00:04:29.107
A, author name. B, context. C, title only.

75
00:04:32.667 --> 00:04:34.736
The answer is B: context.

76
00:04:35.883 --> 00:04:37.860
The writer wants readers to blank.

77
00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.103
A, buy cars. B, stay home. C, demand better roads.

78
00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:48.432
The answer is C: demand better roads.

79
00:04:49.583 --> 00:04:51.351
Facts and numbers show blank.

80
00:04:51.651 --> 00:04:55.536
A, information. B, entertainment. C, fear.

81
00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.436
The answer is A: information.

82
00:05:02.583 --> 00:05:04.583
Why did two drivers refuse?

83
00:05:04.883 --> 00:05:09.291
A, they were ill. B, the road was bad. C, it was night.

84
00:05:12.833 --> 00:05:15.553
The answer is B: the road was bad.

85
00:05:16.767 --> 00:05:20.127
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:20.983 --> 00:05:23.018
How do you answer an inference question?

87
00:05:31.817 --> 00:05:33.387
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:33.687 --> 00:05:44.202
I look for clues in the text and think about what they suggest. I do not use my own opinion. I choose the answer that the evidence supports.

89
00:05:44.502 --> 00:05:46.130
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:05:47.017 --> 00:05:51.016
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and clues to inference.

91
00:05:51.316 --> 00:05:56.104
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

92
00:05:56.404 --> 00:05:59.138
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

93
00:05:59.438 --> 00:06:02.868
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

